# Persistent Context Harness — configuration
#
# Copy to config/harness.env and edit. harness.env is gitignored so your
# machine-specific paths never get committed.
#
#   cp config/harness.env.example config/harness.env
#
# `install/bootstrap.sh` writes this file for you; edit it afterwards to taste.
#
# ── FORMAT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This file is READ, not sourced. It is parsed by BOTH bin/_common.sh and
# lib/harness_config.py using the same strict grammar:
#
#   KEY=value                 whole-line '#' comments; blank lines ignored
#   KEY="value with spaces"   optional surrounding single or double quotes
#   export KEY=value          a leading `export ` is accepted
#
# Values are LITERAL. No command substitution, no escapes, no variable
# expansion, no line continuations — with ONE exception: a leading `~/`,
# `$HOME/` or `${HOME}/` is expanded. Anything else containing `$` is used
# verbatim and warns.
#
# Why so strict: an earlier version was sourced by the shell and parsed by
# Python, and the two disagreed about quoting, escapes and expansion — one
# valid file could give a shell hook different roots than a Python hook.
#
# Every setting below can also be given as an environment variable, which
# takes precedence over this file. Commented lines show the DEFAULT value.

# ── Knowledge base ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the knowledge base lives. Defaults to this repo's example wiki/, so a
# fresh clone works before you have any content. For real notes, point this at
# a SEPARATE, private git repo.
#   KB_ROOT=~/my-kb/wiki
# KB_ROOT=

# Colon-separated project roots where priming and recall should fire. When an
# agent session opens inside one of these (or a subdirectory), the session-start
# hook loads context and the prompt hook enables lesson recall.
#   KB_WORK_ROOTS=~/code/service-a:~/code/service-b
# KB_WORK_ROOTS=$HOME

# Extra directories where retrieval must stay silent, colon-separated.
# KB_ROOT itself is ALWAYS excluded and does not need listing here.
# KB_EXCLUDED_ROOTS=

# For per-root metadata (label, system_slug, enabled) use the optional richer
# file instead: $KB_ROOT/reference/project-roots.json. It takes precedence over
# KB_WORK_ROOTS.

# ── Generated state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Vector index, retrieval log, weekly drafts and run logs. Kept outside KB_ROOT
# on purpose: machine state, not knowledge. Never commit it to your KB.
# KB_STATE_DIR=<repo>/state

# ── Retrieval ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "semantic" (recommended) or "keyword".
#
# Semantic embeds your prompt and runs vector kNN against the lesson index. It
# exists because keyword scoring false-positives badly on generic technical
# words ("turn", "work", "rate"), surfacing unrelated lessons. Semantic needs
# an embedding backend (Ollama by default) — small, fast, and your prompts
# never leave the machine.
#
# Keyword needs nothing and is the automatic fallback whenever the backend is
# unreachable or the index is unhealthy, so recall degrades rather than
# disappears.
KB_RETRIEVER=semantic

# KB_EMBED_URL=http://localhost:11434/api/embed
# KB_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# KB_EMBED_DIM=768

# Interpreter with the extras installed (requests, sqlite-vec) used by the
# semantic index and the weekly pipeline. The installer sets this to its venv.
# Leave unset if you only want the zero-dependency keyword path.
# KB_PYTHON=

# ── Weekly self-improvement ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Agent CLI that performs the weekly reasoning passes (lesson audit, hot-context
# promotions, cross-link drafting, gap triage). Must accept a prompt on stdin in
# headless mode.
#
# This replaces pinning a specific local LLM tag. That approach is fragile: if
# the model is removed or renamed the weekly job fails soft and can stay broken
# for weeks without telling you.
# KB_WEEKLY_AGENT=claude

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "auto" picks macOS Keychain on Darwin, libsecret on Linux.
# KB_SECRET_BACKEND=auto

# Service name used to namespace secrets in the OS keychain.
# KB_SECRET_SERVICE=context-harness
